1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:09,487
In December nineteen hundred, a German physicist named Max Planck stood in front of a meeting of the German Physical Society and proposed that energy comes in lumps.

2
00:00:09,487 --> 00:00:10,737
He did not believe it.

3
00:00:10,737 --> 00:00:12,037
He thought it was a trick.

4
00:00:12,037 --> 00:00:17,937
A mathematical convenience, invented to fix a curve that the physics of his day could not explain.

5
00:00:17,937 --> 00:00:23,237
A hot object glows red, then orange, then white, and nobody could say why.

6
00:00:23,237 --> 00:00:29,825
Planck found a formula that worked, but only if he assumed that energy is handed out in indivisible packets.

7
00:00:29,825 --> 00:00:31,087
He called them quanta.

8
00:00:31,087 --> 00:00:33,550
Then he spent years trying to make them go away.

9
00:00:33,550 --> 00:00:34,812
They did not go away.

10
00:00:34,812 --> 00:00:41,412
Within twenty-five years, that trick had become quantum mechanics: the most accurate theory ever written down.

11
00:00:41,412 --> 00:00:44,162
More accurate than anything else in science.

12
00:00:44,162 --> 00:00:46,687
Tested to one part in a hundred billion.

13
00:00:46,687 --> 00:00:51,387
And still, a century later, nobody can tell you what it actually means.

14
00:00:51,387 --> 00:00:53,175
That is where this film begins.

15
00:00:53,175 --> 00:00:54,512
Not with computers.

16
00:00:54,512 --> 00:00:55,550
With a question.

17
00:00:55,550 --> 00:01:01,462
Is quantum mechanics a description of reality, or is it just the best bookkeeping we have ever managed?

18
00:01:01,462 --> 00:01:06,487
Does the universe really behave this way, or does the maths only pretend that it does?

19
00:01:06,487 --> 00:01:10,662
Because something strange happened while physicists argued about that question.

20
00:01:10,662 --> 00:01:14,600
We stopped asking what the theory means, and started asking what it can do.

21
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:18,937
And the answer turned out to be: almost anything, if you can build the machine.

22
00:01:18,937 --> 00:01:21,775
A quantum computer is not a faster computer.

23
00:01:21,775 --> 00:01:23,375
It is a different kind of thing.

24
00:01:23,375 --> 00:01:25,250
It does not compute with numbers.

25
00:01:25,250 --> 00:01:28,287
It computes with the rules that govern matter itself.

26
00:01:28,287 --> 00:01:30,337
This is the story of how we got there.

27
00:01:30,337 --> 00:01:34,350
The people, the arguments, the photographs, the failures.

28
00:01:34,350 --> 00:01:36,237
And the machine we are building now.

29
00:01:36,237 --> 00:01:39,962
A machine that borrows the universe, and gives it back an answer.

